{
  "title": "Stage 3: Course rankings",
  "summary": "Rank each course with OrderByDescending and ThenBy, keep ties in the top 3, and find the lowest with MinBy.",
  "minutes": 15,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Briefing: who tops each course?",
      "body": [
        "Next the gradebook ranks every course. Sorting by more than one thing is a chain: `OrderByDescending` for the main key, `ThenBy` to break ties.",
        "",
        "```csharp fragment",
        "var ranked = course",
        "    .OrderByDescending(s => s.Points)   // best first",
        "    .ThenBy(s => s.Student)             // ties: alphabetical",
        "    .ToList();",
        "```",
        "",
        "\"Top 3\" sounds like `Take(3)`, but `Take` is blind to ties: if 3rd place shares a score with 4th, `Take(3)` silently drops someone. A fairer rule is *everyone who scored at least the 3rd-best score*:",
        "",
        "```csharp fragment",
        "var cutoff = ranked.Take(3).Last().Points;",
        "var top = ranked.TakeWhile(s => s.Points >= cutoff);",
        "```",
        "",
        "Tied students share a rank: one plus the number of people who did strictly better, `1 + ranked.Count(o => o.Points > s.Points)`.",
        "",
        "`MinBy` and `MaxBy` return the **item** with the smallest or largest key, not just the number, so you can print who it was:",
        "",
        "```csharp fragment",
        "var lowest = ranked.MinBy(s => s.Points)!;   // the whole Score",
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "Sort by score, then by name. What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "var scores = new[] { (\"Dee\", 88), (\"Ada\", 91), (\"Cy\", 88), (\"Eve\", 59) };",
        "",
        "var ranked = scores",
        "    .OrderByDescending(s => s.Item2)",
        "    .ThenBy(s => s.Item1);",
        "",
        "Console.WriteLine(string.Join(\" \", ranked.Select(s => s.Item1)));"
      ],
      "answer": "Ada Cy Dee Eve",
      "explain": "Ada has the best score. Cy and Dee tie on 88, so `ThenBy` puts them alphabetically. Eve is last."
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "`Take` versus `TakeWhile` on a tie. What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "var points = new[] { 97, 88, 84, 84, 70 };",
        "",
        "var take = points.Take(3);",
        "var cutoff = points.Take(3).Last();",
        "var fair = points.TakeWhile(p => p >= cutoff);",
        "",
        "Console.WriteLine(string.Join(\",\", take));",
        "Console.WriteLine(string.Join(\",\", fair));"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "97,88,84",
        "97,88,84,84"
      ],
      "explain": "`Take(3)` stops at three items and drops the second 84. The cutoff is the third score, 84, and `TakeWhile` keeps going while scores reach it, so both 84s make the list."
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "What is the difference between `scores.Min(s => s.Points)` and `scores.MinBy(s => s.Points)`?",
      "options": [
        "`Min` returns the lowest number; `MinBy` returns the `Score` that has it",
        "They are the same; `MinBy` is just the newer name",
        "`MinBy` sorts the list first, `Min` does not",
        "`Min` returns the first item, `MinBy` returns the last"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explain": "`Min` gives you `55`. `MinBy` gives you the whole `Score` with 55 points, so you can say *who*. On ties it returns the first one it meets."
    },
